From: gt510 <gt510@localhost>
Subject: aperture: take stills at the camera's full resolution

Pictures were taken from the viewfinder stream, which best_mode() keeps at
most 1080 lines tall, so a 5 MP camera saved ~1 MP photos. On the shutter,
switch aperture's own capsfilter (between the camera source and decodebin3)
to the largest size the camera offers, start camerabin's capture once caps
of that size arrive, and switch back to the viewfinder caps when the image
is done (reconfigure on shutter). camerabin's image-capture-caps alone does
not work: decodebin3 does not pass its renegotiation upstream ("new caps
equal current ones", then not-negotiated). On the SM-T550 the software ISP
already reads the sensor's full 2592x1944 mode for the preview, so exposure
and white balance carry over; a 2584x1944 still takes ~2.5 s.

APERTURE_MAX_HEIGHT=<lines> lowers the viewfinder's height limit (default
1080), so the preview can stay at its old, cheaper size once larger modes
are offered.

The still caps are the camera device's own structures of that size (no frame-rate
limit added), so they equal the format PipeWire then runs: pipewiresrc only
skips a renegotiation when its preferred new caps equal the current ones, and
with bare width/height caps every renegotiation camerabin triggers during the
capture restarted the camera (4 libcamera reconfigurations per shot).
